{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgiZ3VtMJLHNRxUA1a47f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012FGoPc58LBCoLWJ9bPvsgJ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"~/.claude/projects/-private-tmp-claude-502-<proj>/memory/weight-edit-lab-gated-pathway.md","content":"---\nname: weight-edit-lab-gated-pathway\ndescription: What worked and what failed when hand-editing the Laya scorer (weight-edit-lab): slot gates must be pairwise and leak-checked; topic directions are limited by linear readout, not by data size\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\nLaya weight-edit lab (job-app/weight-edit-lab, steps run 2026-11-04): corrections are added as block-diagonal ReLU pathways `K * AND(slot gates, topic gate)` on standardized PCs of my own marker vectors.\n\n- A pooled \"slot minus others\" gate leaked onto other slots for ~0.1% of held-out points and silently changed a step-1 evidence routing. Pairwise \"slot minus slot j\" ramps, each anchored above the max of slot j (+0.5 sd margin), gave zero leak.\n- Topic held-out quality did not improve from 115 to 230 targets; sparse PC selection and balanced negatives made it worse. The limit is the linear readout in PCs 8-255, so spend effort on gate structure (OR of sub-topic directions, thresholds at the contrast 99th percentile), not on more messages.\n- Earlier split-half numbers from projection statistics looked hopeless while the end-to-end flip rate was fine. Always evaluate end to end (flips and collateral), with the direction built on one half only.\n\n**Why:** the hidden test scores collateral on ~3000 messages, so a leak that changes one routing per thousand matters as much as a missed target.\n**How to apply:** in any later step of this lab, start from step3/lab.py and final.py, keep the leak assertion, and report held-out flip/collateral before choosing a configuration. See [[learn-from-every-mistake]].\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,